# No Test-Suite Coverage for Environment Construction

## Rule

Do not add Minitest or Vitest cases whose subject is the construction of the development or
operational environment. This is a prohibition, not a preference, and it has no "unless the change
is risky" escape hatch.

Environment construction means, at minimum:

- adding, configuring, or mounting a third-party dashboard, engine, or Rack app
- gem, npm, or container dependency changes, and lockfile contents
- `config/environments/*`, `config/application.rb`, initializer wiring for a tool
- Host Authorization entries, route host constraints for an operator surface
- Compose files, devcontainer configuration, Procfiles, network aliases
- build, bundling, linting, and formatting tool configuration
- environment variable plumbing for any of the above

When the work is environment construction, the test suite is not the place the result is recorded.
Run the thing, observe it, and write what you observed into `evidence/`.

## Why

The suite is read as a description of this application's behaviour. Every case in it is a claim
about what the product does, and its value comes from that being true of every case. Setup tests
dilute exactly that: a reader scanning `test/security/invariants/` to learn what the authorization
model guarantees now has to sort real invariants from assertions that a gem was added to the right
Bundler group.

They are also weak evidence dressed as strong evidence. A test asserting that a dashboard is
mounted behind a host constraint passes in an environment where the gem is not even loaded — it
proves the route table looks a certain way under `RAILS_ENV=test`, which is not the environment
anyone was worried about. The check that would have caught the real failure is reaching the host
and watching it answer 401.

And they impose a permanent cost for a one-time activity. Setup happens once; the case runs on
every commit forever, and it breaks on the next upgrade of a gem whose internals it reached into.

## What to do instead

- Run it. Boot the application, open the surface, send the request, watch the failure mode.
- Record what was actually observed in `evidence/`, following the `evidence/` rules in AGENTS.md:
  the commands, the identifiers, the status codes, the excerpts. A check that could not be
  completed is recorded as not completed, with the reason.
- Put the reasoning where the next reader will be standing: a comment in the configuration file
  itself, and an ADR when the decision is architectural.
- If a gem internal was copied into this repository, say so in a comment at the copy site and name
  what to re-read on upgrade.

## Boundary

This rule is about the *subject* of a test, not about which directory it lives in.

- A behaviour change to this application's own code still needs risk-appropriate tests. That
  obligation is unchanged, and "it touched a config file" does not exempt it.
- Existing invariant tests under `test/security/invariants/` stay. Do not extend them to cover a
  newly added tool, and do not delete one because it looks like setup — several encode genuine
  security properties of application code.
- A tool whose output the application depends on at runtime is covered through that dependency, not
  through the tool. The OpenAPI descriptions are contract-tested because the API must match them,
  not because Redocly is configured correctly.

## Accepted consequence

Regressions in environment construction are caught by running it, by review, and by the comments
and ADRs left at the decision sites — not automatically. That trade is deliberate: see
`adr/no-test-suite-for-environment-construction.md`.
